From bf8567eb4bbc6579c747f1d90b27caf37d107d54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 19:32:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 119/206] fix: the ten failures that only a Debug build could
 see

All 160 of our tests were only ever run in ReleaseARM64, where NDEBUG deletes
every ASSERT. Running them in the Debug simulator tree turned up ten failures at
five assertion sites. The verdicts split both ways -- two are production bugs
release was silently tolerating, three are tests asserting what only one
configuration makes true -- so neither answer could have been assumed.

Production, WrapperAllocator::AllocateWrapperPage: it always COW-remapped the
stub's page and DuplicateRX's whole promise is an RX alias. Page::Allocate drops
kExecutable whenever FLAG_use_simulator is on, so the source is R-- and vm_remap
returns VM_PROT_READ; upstream's postcondition then fails. Not a corner case --
wrappers only exist while simulating, so the remap never delivered what it
promised in any configuration the production caller runs in. Now decided by
reading the source mapping's protection, which is the precondition
virtual_memory.h:122 actually states, and falling back to copying the bytes the
way upstream's non-remap branch does. Gating on FLAG_use_simulator instead was
tried and is wrong: it breaks the case where the stub range points into the host
binary's own __TEXT.

Production, link_info.cc's readers: every bound was PendingBytes() > 0, which
promises one byte, and a varint is one to ten. Input that ends mid-varint has no
terminator to stop the decoder, so ReadByte walked off the end -- an ASSERT in
Debug, an out-of-bounds read in release, which is where these run. Bounded now
by looking for the terminator first.

Tests: the three FieldHasher cases borrowed a helper from profiler_test.cc whose
TransitionVMToNative needs kThreadInVM, and TEST_CASE bodies run in
kThreadNative; they are ISOLATE_UNIT_TEST_CASE now, as their callers there are.
The serialization round-trip fed the serializer a graph that still allowed LICM
and, once past that, a graph owned by FlowGraphBuilderHelper's synthetic
function, which is in nobody's function list -- so no graph from that helper can
be serialized at all. It builds its own over a real function now.

And the worst of them was not the assert but what it uncovered: the two Image
range tests were reading dart::bin::vm_snapshot_instructions as an Image. That
blob is 0 bytes in a JIT build, so the symbol is whatever the linker put next --
in ReleaseARM64 it resolves to dart::bin::DFE::~DFE(), and the tests were reading
`stp x20, x19, [sp, #-0x20]!` as ImageSize. They asserted `end > start` about a
2.6 GB range starting at a random function, and passed. Both now build a real
64-byte-aligned image and assert the exact end.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

